## Shared types
- `LessonMeta`: `slug`, `title`, `description`, `order`, `duration`, `tags`, `advancedAvailable`.
- `LessonModule`: metadata plus lazy MDX component import and optional simulation scenario config.
- `ProgressState`: per-lesson completion, quiz results, advanced mode, presentation mode preferences.
- `SimulationScenario`: declarative scene description for bodies, pulleys, anchors, loads, overlays, and readouts.
- `SimulationMetrics`: tension, force, displacement, mechanical advantage, velocity, selected body state.
- `Quiz` model types for multiple-choice, drag-match, and numeric answers with shared correctness/result schema.

## Core design decisions
- Use MDX for lessons so instructional content stays separate from React logic, while still embedding widgets like `<Quiz>` and `<SimulationCanvas>`.
- Keep lesson registry explicit rather than filesystem-magic at runtime; the registry becomes the single source of ordering, metadata, and routes.
- Wrap Matter.js in `lib/physics` and `components/simulation` so lessons consume scenario configs instead of raw engine objects.
- Put persisted user state in a single Zustand store with versioned localStorage persistence to avoid prop drilling and enable progress indicators globally.
- Keep present/teacher mode as a route/query-state concern layered onto the lesson shell rather than forking separate pages.
- Prefer accessible shadcn/ui primitives and Tailwind theme tokens so WCAG/mobile passes are incremental instead of a late rewrite.

## Issue dependency map
- Foundation: `#1 -> #2 -> #3`.
- Content/runtime shell: `#4`, `#5`, `#6` depend on foundation.
- Deployment/dev tooling: `#7`, `#8` depend on foundation; `#8` also depends on `#4`.
- Physics platform: `#9 -> #10 -> #11 -> #12`, with `#13` optional after `#9`.
- Learning widgets: `#14`, `#15`, `#16` can proceed after `#2`, `#3`, `#6`; `#17` depends on `#9-#11`; `#18` depends on `#9` and benefits from `#17`; `#19` depends on `#6`.
- Experience pages: `#20` depends on `#5` and `#9`; `#21` depends on `#5` and `#6`; `#22` depends on `#4`, `#5`, `#6`, and widget groundwork; `#23` depends on `#22`.
- Lesson authoring: `#24-#28` depend on `#4`, `#22`, and whichever widgets/sim features each lesson needs.
- Quality passes: `#29` and `#30` should start after `#20-#28` are mostly present; `#31` should begin once core widgets and pages exist; `#32` depends on `#4` and `#8`; `#33` is last after most implementation lands.

## Recommended implementation order beyond issue numbering
1. Complete `#1-#6` before building any lesson content.
2. Build `#9-#12` before lesson simulations so lesson issues only compose existing primitives.
3. Implement `#14-#19` before `#24-#28` for the same reason.
4. Tackle `#20-#23` before lessons to stabilize page shells and presentation behavior.
5. Reserve `#29-#33` for after feature completeness, but add tests opportunistically as widgets land.
